we've looked at creating dashboards to  visualize the health of our systems  and automating them using the api and  terraform but today  i'm here to show you how to use the new  dashboard editor to visualize your cloud  monitoring data like never before  this is a stackdoctor  [Music]  before we start let's quickly review  what makes for a good dashboard  generally you want to focus on the key  signals that represent the health of  your service  these are covered in the monitoring  distributed systems chapter of the sre  book and we call them the golden signals  you can automate creating a dashboard  using the api or terraform  but today i want to show you how to use  the new dashboard editor in the cloud  console  right away you can see that the new  editor looks very different there's now  a list of widgets on the right to pick  from and a grid for laying out your  dashboard in the main panel  i specifically want to show you three  things the new widgets the new layout  options  and the new chart configuration options  let's dig in  start by adding a widget to the  dashboard the gauge widget finally gives  you a way to show a single number on a  dashboard as an indicator of health  for example you can use it to show mean  cpu limit use of all containers in the  workspace  one thing i really love about this new  widget is the ability to specify the  expected  range for the metric for example you can  set a warning threshold that will turn  the chart yellow  or a danger threshold that will turn it  red this gauge is a good way to  represent the saturation signal  if the containers on average are not  close to the limit there should be  plenty of capacity left  if you care about a specific service you  could filter for just the resources you  need  a gauge is a great way to see the  current value of a metric but it doesn't  show you any historical data if you need  that  you can use the new scorecard widget  this is a great way to visualize a  signal like latency  you can also use the warning and danger  thresholds to change the color of the  chart when the value exceeds the  threshold  this is great to let you know at a  glance that something is wrong  if you just want to see the current  state of a metric and what range it's in  the widget can be toggled to not show  you the time series chart behind it  this way you can see just the current  state of a metric and whether or not  it's within a good range  the last new widget i want to show you  is text yes text  you might be thinking sure you can now  add text boxes to dashboards that's  fine but here's the cool thing about  this  the text widget supports markdown which  lets you format the text  add links and generally make it look  like exactly what you want  this is a great way to add in-context  documentation to your dashboard  these new widgets are great but what can  you do with them let's have a look at  the new way to layout your dashboard  there are now two options for layout  grid the rows and columns you're used to  and mosaic the mosaic option lets you  size each chart individually and place  them where you want on the dashboard  you can use this to create a dashboard  like this showing your service traffic  as measured by request across the top  with the column of health indicators on  the left and documentation on the right  this is a great way to present  information  the last thing i want to show you is the  new way to configure the charts on the  dashboard  when creating a chart you now have the  option of switching between the basic  mode which should be enough for most  users most of the time  and advanced mode for example we can  switch to the advanced mode for a heat  map chart to specify exactly how we want  the data to be pre-processed  and which aligner to use if we need that  this gives you total control over how  the charts interpret  and present data thanks for joining me  today  we took a look at the new dashboard  editor and saw how to use it to create a  dashboard using the gauge  scorecard and text widgets we also  checked out the new layout options and  advanced chart configuration mode  now you're ready to use it to create  your dashboards and keep an eye on the  health of your services  thanks for watching and come back soon  for more this is a stackdoctor  stay healthy out there
 

